  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nOur approach centers on adaptive multi-resolution optimization with coordinated perturbations and filtered gradient descent. Unlike standard SLSQP methods, we use a physics-inspired phase-oscillator model to guide perturbations. The method intelligently balances exploration/exploitation by (1) dynamically adjusting grid resolution based on correlation profiles, (2) injecting low-frequency noise to escape local optima, and (3) using gradient information to sculpt function shapes that minimize maximum overlap. This differs fundamentally from previous works that relied on fixed grid smoother schedules.\n\nMathematical Specification\nWe optimize h(x) : [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] with \u222bh(x)dx=1 to minimize:\nC\u2085 = (1/(k_max+1)) * max_{k=0}^{k_max} \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)L(x+k)dx\nWhere L(x) = 1-h(x) (overlap integrator).\n\nDiscretization: N grid points gives dx = 2/N. We model h with Fourier components:\nh(x) = 0.5 + \u2211_{j=1}^{J} (A_j sin(j\u03c9x) + B_j cos(j\u03c9x))\n\nConstraints are embedded via projection:\nProjection(h): \n  h_proj = clip(h,0,1)\n  total_proj = \u2211h_proj\n  if abs(total_proj - N/2) < 1e-6: return h_proj\n  else: return h_proj * (N/2 / total_proj) clipped to [0,1]\n\nImplementation Procedure\nThe algorithm consists of six phases:\n\n1. Initialization\n   - Start with provided initial (if available) or sinusoidal bump function\n   - Project to valid set\n\n2. Perturbation Phase\n   - Generate candidate perturbations based on gradient information\n     g(x) = E_k [h(x+k)L(x+k)] - E_k [h(x)L(x+k)] (filtered covariance)\n   - Create candidates via:\n        h_new(x) = h(x) + \u03c3 * g(x) * N(0,1) \n        followed by projection\n\n3. Coordinated Descent\n   - Optimizes through gradient mapping:\n      \u2207_i h \u21a6 -\u2202L/\u2202h_i + \u03bb (\u2202/\u2202h_i [\u222bh dx - 1])\n   - Uses accelerated gradient scheme with adaptive damping\n\n4. Resolution Transfer\n   - Transfer parent solution to new grid via:\n        x_new = np.linspace(0,2,n_new)\n        h_new = np.interp(x_new, x_old, h_old)\n   - Filter for PA (Phase Alignment) by normalizing means/quantiles\n\n5. Objective Surrogate\n   - Smooth energy function: J_T(h) = max_k E_k + (1/T)log(\u2211_m exp(T(E_m - E_k)))\n   - SLSQP used with heuristic stopping when correlation gap < 1e-6\n\n6. Cycle Management\n   - Dynamic resolution decision: \n        dC_max = |prior_C - current_max_overlap|\n        if dC_max < 1e-4 * prior_C: increase resolution\n        if progress stalls: inject correlated noise (not plain Gaussian)\n\nSearch Choices and Useful Variations\n1. Perturbation Magnitude Schedule:\n   \u03c3(k) = max(0.001*log(k), 0.005) with k being SLSQP iteration\n2. Temperature For Surrogate: T adapts between [1000, 20000] based on progress\n3. Termination Criteria:\n   - Time-based: checkpoint every 5% of budget\n   - C5-based: gap < 0.995 * prior_best_C\n   - Failure: no improvement in 10 consecutive SLSQP runs\n\nComputational Budget and Robustness\nPhase time allocation:\n- Phase 0: 5% for evaluation\n- Phase 1-4: 85% total, distributed as:\n   - SLSQP runs: 30%\n   - Perturbation: 15%\n   - Grid transfer: 15%\n- Fallback: stored intermediate best\n\nValidation and Return Contract\n1. Validate final solution:\n   a. Check \u2211h = N/2 with 1e-6 tolerance\n   b. Check all h_i in [0,1]\n   c. Recompute C5 to verify value\n\n2. Sanitary checks:\n   - If any derivative warning: scale gradients by 1e-3 for stability\n   - If correlation computation expects inf: clip derivatives to [-1e4, 1e4]\n\n3. Phase reset trigger:\n   - Early exit if time > 0.95*total_time\n   - Fallback to stored best\n\nReturn tuple (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n\nResolution changes:\n- From 160 to 256: use cubic interpolation, validate conservation\n- 256 to 512: \n   new_h = interp(old_x, new_x, old_h)\n   threshold = np.quantile(new_h, 0.15)\n   new_h = np.clip(new_h - threshold, 0, 1)\n   scale = 512/2 / (np.sum(new_h)*dx)\n   return new_h * scale\n\nBest regards,\n\n[Implementation Plan]",
